narrative:
## Building a Number Guessing Game

### Game Flow

1. Pick a secret number (1-100)
2. Loop: read a guess, compare, give a hint
3. Track the number of attempts
4. When correct: celebrate and show the score

### Implementation

The runnable version below reads your guesses with `io.read_int()`.
`io.read_int()` returns a `Result`: `Ok(value)` when the line was a number,
`Err(message)` when it was not (or the input ran out).

```xiom
use xiom.io;

fn main() -> Int {
  let secret = 42;
  io.println("Guess a number between 1 and 100!");

  var attempts = 0;
  var done = false;
  while !done && attempts < 10 {
    let parsed = io.read_int();
    match parsed {
      Ok(guess) => {
        attempts += 1;
        if guess == secret {
          io.println("Correct! Attempts: " + attempts.to_str());
          done = true;
        } else {
          if guess < secret {
            io.println("Too low!");
          } else {
            io.println("Too high!");
          };
        };
      }
      Err(message) => {
        io.println("I could not read that: " + message);
        done = true;
      };
    };
  };
  return 0;
}
```

### Score Calculation

```xiom
fn calculate_score(attempts: Int) -> Int {
  let score = (7 - attempts + 1) * 100 / 7;
  if score < 0 { return 0; };
  score
}
```

### Playing for real

This lesson ships sample input: a complete winning game (7 guesses).
The Input box opens automatically - press **Run** and watch the hints,
then edit the box to play it your way. The secret is fixed at 42 so runs
are reproducible; a real game would draw it from the random module.

The perfect strategy is bisection: each guess splits the remaining range
in half, so 1-100 can never take more than 7 guesses.

### Difficulty Levels

| Level | Range | Max Attempts |
|-------|-------|-------------|
| Easy | 1-50 | 10 |
| Medium | 1-100 | 7 |
| Hard | 1-200 | 5 |

